in Navigation, an assemblage of several breadths of canvas sewed together by the lifts, and edged round with cord, fastened to the yards of a ship, to make it drive before the wind. See SHIP.

The edges of the cloths, or pieces, of which a sail is composed, are generally sewed together with a double seam; and the whole is skirted round at the edges with a cord, called the boh rope.

Although the form of sails is extremely different, they are all nevertheless triangular or quadrilateral figures;
